\NeedsTeXFormat{LaTeX2e}
\ProvidesPackage{novelette-settings}[2026-04-25 Novelette Settings]


%% OVERVIEW:
%%   Novelette does not use class options. Instead, you write various settings in the Preamble.
%% The values are stored until AtBeginDocument. Every setting has a default.
%%   When a setting is written, it is immediately sanity-checked for syntax and allowed values.
%% Insane settings throw an error, often fatal. Sane settings are stored.
%%   During AtBeginDocument, all written and default settings are implemented. If there are
%% incompatible settings, error or warning. Implementation is in stages, because the effects
%% of some settings will depend on what came before.
%%   Note that you cannot choose the margins, font, or font size for main text. These are fixed or
%% automatically calculated, based on other settings. This is a unique feature of Novelette.
%% The fixed/calculated values have been carefully chosen for compatibility with nearly all
%% worldwide print-on-demand services, and for readability in popular fiction books.

\newif\ifnvt@duplicatesetting %% No repeated settings allowed. If this becomes true, problem.

%% METADATA:
%%   The \title and \author settings are required. These will be stored in the Info dictionary
%% and (if you use it) XMP metadata.
%%   If your book has a subtitle, you may use \subtitle to store it. This is not shown anywhere
%% in the Info dictionary or XMP, because (believe it or not) there is no standard for it,
%% and your print service does not care. But the value is hashed, along with title and author,
%% to create ID hex strings.
%%   The optional \docdate and \version are also hashed (if used) for ID. If you use \docdate,
%% it will lock the creation date to the start of that day GMT, even if you modify the
%% document later. If you use \version, it will appear as VersionID in XMP (if used).
%% Thus, a document with fixed (or unused) title, author, subtitle, docdate, and version,
%% will have its document ID hash at a fixed value. This may be useful for reproducible builds.
%% However, the instance ID always changes at each compile, even if the document is unchanged.
%%   Novelette has special requirements for title, author, subtitle, docdate, and version.
%% The character set is limited to Latin-1 alphanumeric, with a few characters of common
%% punctuation. Most symbols cannot be used. Quotation marks, of any kind, cannot be used,
%% but the single straight quote may be used as apostophe.
%%   Some (not all) print services allow @ # $ % & characters in metadata. If you use them,
%% they must be preceded by backslash: \@ \# \$ \% \&. Otherwise, the backslash is strictly
%% prohibited, so you cannot use LaTeX codes such as \'e for lowercase eacute; but you may paste
%% its Unicode character. Note that the fonts have many printable characters that are not
%% allowed in metadata, so that a book written in English can have short dialogue in Welsh
%% or Hawai'ian with correct characters.
%%   How it works: Each metadata setting is detokenized, so that it becomes a literal string.
%% At the end of \begin{document}, it is temporarily typeset in a one-line box,
%% using OpenType features that assign \@ \# \$ \% and \& to their plain single characters,
%% and that assign out-of-range characters to a too-deep substitute. If the depth of the box
%% is too deep, then a fatal error is thrown. Error or not, the page is discarded.
%% Later, when metadata is prepared, Lua manipulates those detokenized setting strings.
%%   Traditionally, the colon separates title from subtitle in the book distribution database.
%% This is easy to understand for a book such as "Firble Bottoms : A Detective Smith Mystery".
%% But for books such as "2001 : A Space Odyssey" and "Magic : The Gathering", it is not clear
%% whether those are just the title, or are title : subtitle. If in doubt, ask your distributor.
%%   The numbersign # is traditionally used for books in a series, such as
%% "Doofus Manor : #7 in the Doofus Chronicles". Some distibutors refuse to carry books in
%% a serial, unless they carry all of them. Ask.
%%   Many distributors refuse books with a monetary unit such as $ in the metadata, although they
%% may accept Dollar spelled out. Similarly for % and @ and &. Ask. Fun fact: "The 7% Solution"
%% and "The 7 Per-Cent Solution" are different books, by different authors. One is nonfiction,
%% the other is fiction.
%%   If you use \docdate, its format is yyyy/mm/dd. If you use \version, it may be anything
%% convenient, such as Edition 1.02, build 2394.
%%   The total number of characters in all of title, author, subtitle, docdate, and version,
%% may not exceed 240. This is much more than you need for fiction. Do not use the subtitle
%% as a lengthy description or marketing tool; you can provide that kind of information to the
%% distribution database, without it appearing anywhere in the PDF.
%%   You may use a pseudonym as author. But if you do that, be sure to print your real name
%% as copyright holder, on the copyright page. That avoids problems if someone cheats.
%% If you don't show your real name, you could only enforce copyright by proving that
%% "Aloysius Fakename" is "Real E. You". You cannot hide from the distribution database.
\newif\ifnvt@didtitle
\def\title#1{%%
  \ifnvt@didtitle\nvt@duplicatesettingtrue\fi
  \edef\nvt@rawtitle{\nvt@expdet{#1}}
  \catcode`\#=12 \catcode`\$=12 \catcode`\%=12 \catcode`\&=12
  \directlua{nvt.parsemeta('\luaescapestring{\nvt@rawtitle}', 'title')}
  \catcode`\#=6 \catcode`\$=3 \catcode`\%=14 \catcode`\&=4
  \ifnum\tmpreturn=0\relax
    \nvt@fatal{Bad setting for \string\title. Unmatched parentheses ().}%%
  \else
    \def\thetitle{\nvt@expdet{\tmptitle}}
  \fi
  \nvt@didtitletrue
}%%
\title{Untitled Document}\nvt@didtitlefalse %% Default.
%%
\newif\ifnvt@didauthor
\def\author#1{%%
  \ifnvt@didauthor\nvt@duplicatesettingtrue\fi
  \edef\nvt@rawauthor{\nvt@expdet{#1}}
  \catcode`\#=12 \catcode`\$=12 \catcode`\%=12 \catcode`\&=12
  \directlua{nvt.parsemeta('\luaescapestring{\nvt@rawauthor}', 'author')}
  \catcode`\#=6 \catcode`\$=3 \catcode`\%=14 \catcode`\&=4
  \ifnum\tmpreturn=0\relax
    \nvt@fatal{Bad setting for \string\author. Unmatched parentheses ().}%%
  \else
    \edef\theauthor{\tmpauthor}
  \fi
  \nvt@didauthortrue
}%%
\author{Anonymous Author}\nvt@didauthorfalse %% Default.
%%
